WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.310
Hi, welcome back to the Lam Rim,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:07.310
and you're probably wondering
what is this sewer tops,

00:00:08.778 --> 00:00:10.170
the tops to the sewer?

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.140
And I don't know, maybe I
told you this story before

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.750
but once you get older,

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:19.120
you like to tell stories more.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.700
And also you forget which
story you told people

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.060
and you didn't tell people.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.270
So I apologize if I told
you this story before.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.070
But I used to go to Mongolia a lot

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:35.060
to work in the different libraries there,

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:39.242
which have hundreds of thousands
of great Buddhist books.

00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:40.560
One of the greatest,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.560
some of the greatest Buddhist
libraries in the world.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.610
So I remember one night

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.610
we were coming home from the library,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:53.190
we were walking to the
hotel and it was dark.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.460
They didn't have a lot of street lights

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:01.017
and I could see in front
of us on the street,

00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:03.161
there was something moving,

00:01:03.161 --> 00:01:06.373
and I look closer and
there's this sewer lid,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.620
and it was opening.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.640
There was a small animal
coming out of the sewer

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.760
in the dark, dark night.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.760
Then the animal came out
and the sewer lid fell down,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.030
and then this animal rushed past me

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.610
and almost touched me.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.990
Then I was like, oh.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.310
I asked my friend, my Mongolian friend,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.118
I said, "What kind of animal is that?

00:01:33.118 --> 00:01:36.530
Is it a wolf, it's living
in the sewer or something?

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.760
And they can open the sewer?"

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.700
He said, "No, no, no, that's not a wolf.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.550
That's a kid. That's a human kid."

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.080
And I'm like, what?

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.920
He said, "Yeah, that's a
kid, that's a wild kid.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.780
We call them wild kids."

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.710
I said, "What's a wild kid?"

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:59.070
He said, "Well, if your
parents die in Mongolia,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.980
and if you don't have any other family,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.890
you don't have money,

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.910
then you will live in the sewer.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:06.960
You can live in the sewer."

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.117
I said, "Why they live in the
sewer, in the stinky sewer?"

00:02:10.117 --> 00:02:15.117
They say, "It's cold here,
Geshe-la, the sewer is warm.

00:02:15.127 --> 00:02:17.230
The sewer has shelter.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.700
The snow, you're not
going to sit in the snow,

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.230
and the poo-poo makes the sewer warm,

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.680
and it's a warm place to stay.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:25.860
So the kids will stay there."

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.750
I said, "What?

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.350
But that didn't look like a kid.

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.873
That looked like a wolf or something."

00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:33.760
He said, "Yeah, they are
wild, they are really wild."

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.150
And he said, "Don't get close
to them, they will bite you."

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.010
I'm like, "What?"

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.500
He said, "Yeah, those are wild kids.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.570
They cannot talk. They cannot speak.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.520
They didn't have parents.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.520
And no one taught them
to live like a human,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.870
so they are living like a wolf.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.570
They are living like an animal,

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.320
and if you get close to
them, they'll bite you.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.980
They are like wild animals
and they don't talk.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.063
They steal things from people.

00:03:04.063 --> 00:03:07.400
In fact one of our staff
members had their bag stolen

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.400
by one of these wild kids."

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.400
And I was like, "Wow, that's so strange.:

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.485
Then I started to think.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.010
I would be like that if
my mom didn't train me.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.200
If my mom didn't teach me how to talk,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.890
how to eat, how to put on clothes,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.510
then I would be one of these
naked children running around

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:35.060
and biting tourists in Mongolia.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.160
I would be the same, I'm not special,

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.350
I'm not different from them.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.930
That's what Pabongka
Rinpoche is going say next.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.214
So, DER MA ZAD

00:03:44.214 --> 00:03:47.213
means not only did she take care of us

00:03:47.213 --> 00:03:49.160
and watched over us,

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.340
that we talked about in the last session.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:54.536
Hundreds of times a
day, she saved my life.

00:03:54.536 --> 00:03:56.650
RKANG PA 'DZUG PA TSUN CHAD

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.813
She taught us to take our first steps.

00:03:59.813 --> 00:04:02.630
I remember, so funny,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:06.540
I remember Halloween night, and my mom,

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.320
my dad is teaching me how to chew gum

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.310
and how to blow bubbles,
and they were so excited.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.643
My mom and dad,

00:04:13.643 --> 00:04:16.527
"Look he's blowing a bubble,"
and I was like (blows air).

00:04:16.527 --> 00:04:19.450
They teach you everything.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.097
They teach you how to
take your first steps.

00:04:22.097 --> 00:04:24.470
GTAM SMRA BA means they teach you

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.790
how to say your first words.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.300
I remember my parents being really happy

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.728
when I learned a new word or something.

00:04:31.728 --> 00:04:35.130
ZAS ZA STANGS how to eat

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.130
with a fork and a spoon or chopsticks

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.510
and don't eat with your fingers.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.500
Your parents taught you that.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.330
Even those, forget university,

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:49.343
forget reading Cervantes or Shakespeare,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.320
just to eat without your fingers,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.357
they taught you everything.

00:04:55.357 --> 00:04:59.283
TSUN means everything
from that up. TSUN NAS

00:04:59.283 --> 00:05:01.930
RIM BZHIN here's the RIM in Lam Rim,

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:05.170
which means one by one,
they taught you how to step,

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.776
how to talk. BSLAB PAS they taught you.

00:05:08.776 --> 00:05:10.509
DA LTA 'GRO 'DUG DANG,

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:13.901
GTAM SMRA BA SOGS LA
THOGS PA MED PA 'DI BYUNG

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:18.470
That's why nowadays you and
I can stand up and walk,

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:21.085
or sit down politely,

00:05:21.085 --> 00:05:23.280
or GTAM SMRA BA

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.757
we can have a conversation with somebody.

00:05:25.757 --> 00:05:29.507
THOGS PA MED -- Asanga

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.250
What's it got to do
with talking or eating?

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.808
It's fluent.

00:05:35.808 --> 00:05:38.570
It's, we don't have to think about it.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.350
You just start talking THOGS PA MED Asanga

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.880
this THOGS PA MED means,

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.910
how do you say that?

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.888
Without any hesitation.

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:52.231
You can just start a conversation.

00:05:52.231 --> 00:05:56.834
That's all due to them
teaching your first words.

00:05:56.834 --> 00:05:59.509
The fact that you can THOGS PA MED

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:01.672
walk downstairs and get in your car,

00:06:01.672 --> 00:06:03.960
is because they taught you
how to take your first step.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.904
So don't forget.

00:06:06.904 --> 00:06:10.172
MTHAR RANG GI DNGUL KHA GANG TZAM LA'ANG

00:06:10.172 --> 00:06:14.720
Now they're going to talk
about a special subject,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.055
which is the financial burden

00:06:17.055 --> 00:06:20.570
that you were for your parents.

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.710
And it's bad, it's a lot.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.980
I was coming here to the college,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:29.140
I saw a lady driving
out of the parking lot

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.790
and she had two or three kids in the back.

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:36.650
The car was messed up. The
car was old and broken,

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:39.730
and I feel a little bit, offended.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.140
I'm like why you drive the stupid car?

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.362
Then I look at the kids and I realize,

00:06:44.362 --> 00:06:46.700
oh that's why you drive this stupid car,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:50.100
because you have kids and
you can't afford them.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.480
You're not like me. I can spend on,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.740
I can buy a nice car, but she can't. Why?

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:00.220
I think the average cost of
a kid in the United States

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.600
is $30,000 a year.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.290
You get three kids in the car.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.610
I don't care what kind of job you have.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.590
You can't have a new car.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:10.470
You can have a junkie old car.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.419
Because that's $90,000 a
year sitting in the back,

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:16.310
and that's unbelievable.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:19.220
Of course, young people

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.908
don't think about that
before they have a baby.

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:24.910
But it's expensive, it's
extremely expensive.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:29.328
You can't say, don't eat
this week. Or you can't say,

00:07:29.328 --> 00:07:32.185
we're going to send you
to school without shoes.

00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:35.030
You don't have a choice, you
have to spend that money.

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.946
So DNGUL KHA GANG TZAM LA'ANG
to make a single dollar.

00:07:39.946 --> 00:07:42.714
DNGUL KHA means a single silver piece,

00:07:42.714 --> 00:07:44.730
piece of silver.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.750
She did whatever she had to do.

00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:50.253
I remember, you got
that next picture Seiji,

00:07:50.253 --> 00:07:51.570
where's that?

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:55.100
Yeah, it's the garbage truck.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:08:00.100
I remember my mom, she was,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:03.810
I remember what she did to make money.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.643
So in my mom's time, in America,

00:08:07.643 --> 00:08:10.176
my dad was not there.

00:08:10.176 --> 00:08:12.290
They split up,

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.450
and my mom, suddenly
she has to make money,

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.410
and she never made money before.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.610
She didn't know how to make money.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.770
Because this is 1950's, 60's housewife.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.610
She's not supposed to work.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.250
Then suddenly, she has to make money,

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.150
and she's like, oh my god.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.420
There's two jobs for women at that time.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.743
It's a secretary or a school teacher.

00:08:32.743 --> 00:08:35.630
So she decided to be a school teacher.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:37.940
The pay is less than a garbage man.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.400
I remember one day we're complaining,

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.010
me and the four brothers,
we are complaining.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:50.010
Mom, why can't we have the more
expensive breakfast cereal?

00:08:50.795 --> 00:08:52.797
She's like, "We can't afford it."

00:08:52.797 --> 00:08:54.880
And we're like, "Mom, why not?

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.878
Our friends have a expensive
breakfast cereals."

00:08:57.878 --> 00:09:00.100
She's like, and then the
garbage truck came by

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.700
and she said, "Look at that
guy, in the garbage truck."

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.090
And we're like, "Yeah."

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.290
She says, "He makes more
than a school teacher

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:08.247
in America, you understand?"

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.450
She said, "I'm doing my best.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:13.472
This is the only job I can get.

00:09:13.472 --> 00:09:17.355
And I can't, I'm struggling
for every single dollar."

00:09:17.355 --> 00:09:20.096
And that's the way it was.

00:09:21.322 --> 00:09:23.960
DNGUL KHA GANG TZAM LA'ANG SDIG DANG

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.380
Moms will do anything they have to do

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.720
to make that money, including prostitution

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:30.470
or whatever they have to do.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:34.170
Doing any bad thing, they will do it.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.920
For their kids they will do it.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.283
There's many stories in the sutras,

00:09:38.283 --> 00:09:40.600
of mothers who turned to prostitution

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.690
to support their kids or stuff like that.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:48.690
A woman will do any kind of
degrading and disgusting job,

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.740
if it means that she can
give money to her children.

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:52.982
And your mom did the same thing,

00:09:52.982 --> 00:09:55.084
and my mom did the same thing.

00:09:55.084 --> 00:09:56.080
SDIG PA DANG, GTAM NGAN

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.080
She doesn't care what
people say about her.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.233
She won't listen to
other people's opinion.

00:10:03.233 --> 00:10:05.161
If she can make a dollar for you,

00:10:05.161 --> 00:10:07.048
she will make a dollar for you.

00:10:07.048 --> 00:10:08.610
SDUG BSNGAL KHYAD DU BSAD

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.990
She doesn't care how hard she has to work.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.156
My mom worked, oh my gosh,

00:10:13.156 --> 00:10:15.493
she came home from teaching all day

00:10:15.493 --> 00:10:18.595
and then she had about three
hours of work after that,

00:10:18.595 --> 00:10:19.930
to check the papers.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.130
She didn't go to bed until
midnight or something

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.430
and then she got up in the
morning and did it again.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:27.677
They don't care, because
they care about you.

00:10:27.677 --> 00:10:29.745
They don't care about themselves.

00:10:29.745 --> 00:10:32.360
I think my mom killed herself working.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.410
She did it for you.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.460
She doesn't care.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.458
She worked herself to death just for you.

00:10:40.458 --> 00:10:42.483
KHYAD DU BSAD NAS BSAGS PA'I

00:10:42.483 --> 00:10:45.482
NOR RANG GI SHA LAS GCES PAR BZUNG NAS

00:10:45.482 --> 00:10:50.482
Then, when she gets money,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.120
she just thinks about spending it on you.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:55.515
She doesn't think about
spending it on herself.

00:10:55.515 --> 00:10:58.445
I remember in the summer
we went to the ocean.

00:10:58.445 --> 00:11:00.260
I said, "Mom, I need a surfboard."

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:02.155
And she's like, "Are you kidding?

00:11:02.155 --> 00:11:03.850
We don't have any money."

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.097
And I'm like, "Mom, I want a surfboard."

00:11:06.097 --> 00:11:08.624
And she says, "Okay."

00:11:08.624 --> 00:11:11.090
I don't know where she took the money,

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.400
from buying her own clothes
for work or something,

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.960
and she buys a surfboard.
I'll never forget it.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:18.663
We didn't have the money,

00:11:18.663 --> 00:11:23.183
but she took it from
some place. She found it.

00:11:23.183 --> 00:11:26.841
They will always find the
money for their kids, right?

00:11:26.841 --> 00:11:29.640
BU BU MO LA KHYIM 'BOGS PA

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.343
Then later, and this is very
common all over the world.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:40.530
Someone told me yesterday, what was it?

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:43.430
It's a good friend of ours.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.990
He's already planning for his son's house.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:48.670
His son is two months old.

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:50.070
He's like, yeah, we're
going to buy a house,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:50.903
we're going to buy him a house,

00:11:50.903 --> 00:11:51.736
we're going to get a mortgage,

00:11:51.736 --> 00:11:53.340
we're going to take care of the mortgage.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:54.675
He's going to have a house

00:11:54.675 --> 00:11:56.802
when he gets to 30 and
we give him the house.

00:11:56.802 --> 00:12:00.277
I'm like, oh my god, the
kid was born two months ago

00:12:00.277 --> 00:12:03.850
and the parents are already
planning the mortgage

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.860
just for the kid, just to
give the house to the kid.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.930
So your mom, KHYIM 'BOGS PA

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:11.960
means they set you up with your own house

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.516
for your own family.

00:12:13.516 --> 00:12:15.285
MNA' MAR BRDZANGS PA

00:12:15.285 --> 00:12:18.180
means, for girl children in Tibet,

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:20.103
they arrange for your husband,

00:12:20.103 --> 00:12:23.711
they got you married to
a good one, like my wife.

00:12:23.711 --> 00:12:26.472
RAB TU BYUNG BA'I SGOR BCUG

00:12:26.472 --> 00:12:28.638
Or they took you to the monastery

00:12:28.638 --> 00:12:31.220
and made you into a monk or nun.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:35.280
In Tibet, it was one out
of six people was ordained,

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.860
a million people were ordained

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:40.571
by the time of the cultural revolution.

00:12:41.894 --> 00:12:45.672
RANG LA 'KHOS KA GANG YOD
PA'I BKA' DRIN GYIS BSKYANGS

00:12:45.672 --> 00:12:49.460
They took whatever money they
had and they spent it on you.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:53.970
So try to, I know in the modern world

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.490
there's a lot of people

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:59.510
who feel like their parents let them down.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:01.750
They didn't treat them
nice, their parents were...

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:03.810
Why do you think your
parents were fighting?

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:05.662
They were stressed out. Why?

00:13:05.662 --> 00:13:07.940
Because they're trying
to make money for you.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:09.072
Yeah, they fought.

00:13:09.072 --> 00:13:10.397
Yeah, they drank alcohol.

00:13:10.397 --> 00:13:12.100
Yeah, they smoked cigarettes.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:16.330
Because they were exhausted
taking care of you.

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:20.840
So don't be so righteous
about your parents.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:24.970
If they were tired, and grumpy,
and fighting, and drinking,

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.380
it's because of you.

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:29.120
So if you don't have
anything to feel bad about

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:33.063
you can feel bad about that.

00:13:34.017 --> 00:13:37.070
MDOR NA RANG GIS SHES TSAD DANG NUS TSAD

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:40.920
Your mom used every ounce of her brains

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:44.010
and every ounce of her physical energy

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:47.640
to find food and housing for you.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.210
That was her thing. She
didn't do anything else.

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.330
Most of her time was spent

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.730
trying to find these things for you.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:59.180
TSAD DANG NUS TSAD KYIS PHAN
BDE GANG YIN 'BAD PAS SGRUB

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.596
She just struggled to do that.

00:14:01.596 --> 00:14:04.940
GNOD PA DANG SDUG BSNGAL SEL
BAR BYED PA SOGS KYI DRIN TSAD

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:07.090
And on the other hand,

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:11.390
she's trying constantly to
protect you from yourself.

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:14.832
Don't take those drugs.

00:14:14.832 --> 00:14:17.240
Did you have this lecture from your mom?

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.387
Look, you can have a girlfriend,
okay, you can have sex,

00:14:20.387 --> 00:14:23.012
but please learn about birth control.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.825
She tried to protect you from yourself.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:32.930
Like with the drugs, my
mom would say about drugs.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:36.468
She said, "Look, now,
you can't hurt yourself,

00:14:36.468 --> 00:14:40.043
I don't want you to hurt yourself."

00:14:40.043 --> 00:14:45.043
DE BZHIN DU PHA SOGS SEMS
CAN GZHAN THAMS CAD KYIS

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:47.154
KYANG DRIN GYIS BSKYANGS

00:14:47.154 --> 00:14:49.680
Then there are other people in your life.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:52.940
Your father did a lot,
your uncles and aunts,

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.290
your grandparents, your school teachers.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.190
Start to expand the awareness

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.498
of how many people took care of you

00:15:01.498 --> 00:15:03.912
and tried to keep you out of trouble.

00:15:03.912 --> 00:15:07.529
BSKYANGS PA 'DRA BA YIN, DE
LTAR SEMS CAN THAMS CAD KYIS

00:15:07.529 --> 00:15:10.512
MI'I RTEN LA RANG GI MA BYAS
PA GCIG PU 'ANG GRANGS KYIS

00:15:10.512 --> 00:15:11.345
MI CHOD

00:15:11.345 --> 00:15:14.380
Even if you don't count all the times

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:18.073
that you were in a different
body, like a bird or a fish,

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:21.930
even just in your human bodies,

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:24.560
your human mom and your human dad

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.069
took care of you countless times.

00:15:27.069 --> 00:15:32.047
And they did. They purposely

00:15:32.047 --> 00:15:36.097
and voluntarily took on the
struggle to support you.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.750
And of course it's an emotional struggle,

00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:42.190
of course it's a physical struggle,

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:44.200
but it's a financial struggle also.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:47.760
And they decided to do it.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.926
They decided to have a baby, mostly.

00:15:50.926 --> 00:15:55.255
DE'I TSE YANG DA LTA'I MA
BZHIN DRIN GYIS BSKYANGS

00:15:55.255 --> 00:15:58.900
And you're even, DA LTA'I MA

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:03.900
in those past lives, your present
mother did the same thing.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.660
My mom who struggled in this life

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:08.950
to make money for me in this life,

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:12.426
also struggled to make money
for me in my past lives.

00:16:12.426 --> 00:16:17.120
DE BZHIN DU 'GRO BA GZHAN GYI
MA BYAS SKABS KYANG RI DVAGS

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:20.290
Then you can start going,

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:23.560
expanding the circle of your gratitude

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:27.920
to the times when this
same woman took care of you

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:31.510
in a different body, like a horse,

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.880
or like a deer, or something.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:38.613
So now recognizing that in a human body,

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:43.250
she struggled for the money for you

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.380
in a human body countless times.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:48.360
Let's start going to the other bodies

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:50.528
in which she did the same for you.

00:16:50.528 --> 00:16:53.130
And Pabongka Rinpoche takes the example

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.920
of a wild animal, like a deer.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:01.063
In that wild animal's
body, DE'I MAR BYAS TE

00:17:01.063 --> 00:17:02.934
she served as your mom,

00:17:02.934 --> 00:17:04.980
BYAMS PA'I LAS THA NA'ANG

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:08.350
and she showed you the same kind of love

00:17:08.350 --> 00:17:11.809
that this human mothers did.

00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:14.775
THA NA'ANG even down to,

00:17:14.775 --> 00:17:16.041
LCES BLDAG PA

00:17:16.041 --> 00:17:18.810
means to lick your body with her tongue.

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.427
And we have a picture of that I think.

00:17:24.370 --> 00:17:29.370
I was looking for a picture
of a cow licking her calf,

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:34.490
but I ran into this camel picture

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:37.220
and it was kind of more powerful.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:40.200
So this little camel was just born

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:45.200
and the mom is licking the
liquids off of the baby,

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:50.250
just after birth. She has this instinct.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:55.020
So try to think of how
many millions of times

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.840
your mom licked the afterbirth off of you

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:00.959
as an animal.

00:18:00.959 --> 00:18:02.910
BLDAG PA TSUN CHAD KYIS BYAMS PAR BSKYANGS

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:07.560
That was the beginning of her career

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:10.740
of affection for you for a whole lifetime,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:13.220
as a camel, for example.

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:17.110
BYA LTA BU'I MA BYAS SKABS

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:20.790
Let's discuss times when
your mom took care of you

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:22.438
as a bird.

00:18:22.438 --> 00:18:26.263
You were a baby bird
and she was a mama bird.

00:18:26.263 --> 00:18:28.401
MA BYAS SKABS KYANG

00:18:28.401 --> 00:18:33.401
ZLA BA GCIG TZAM GSHOG PAS RUM

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:37.430
She spent a month keeping you warm,

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:41.910
both as an egg and as a chick.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:45.530
So in the nest, she laid there

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:48.470
with her wings purposely covering the egg,

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:50.280
even before you were born.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:52.506
Then after you were born, RUM here

00:18:52.506 --> 00:18:56.940
means like, what do you
call that inner fire?

00:18:58.181 --> 00:19:00.923
GTUM MO, it means GTUM MO.

00:19:00.923 --> 00:19:04.043
She kept you warm with her body warmth.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.597
Both as an egg and after
you came out of the egg.

00:19:08.597 --> 00:19:09.600
MIS DBYUG PA SOGS KYIS

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:12.730
And if, and I remember this,

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:16.050
so I had a kid who lived next door to us

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.930
when I was a small kid, and he was bad.

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:22.190
He was just a bad kid. He was a mean kid.

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:25.913
He would say, "Let's go kill birds."

00:19:27.050 --> 00:19:28.808
I went with him once

00:19:28.808 --> 00:19:31.830
and he would take a small gun,

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:35.640
BB gun, or something like that.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:40.640
And he would go find nests
and he would shoot the bird.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:46.870
If the bird had kids in
the nest, she will not fly.

00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:49.890
She will cover the
babies and she will die.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.170
She will purposely die.

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:54.460
She knows she's going to die,
but she will cover the babies

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:56.150
and she will wait for the gun.

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:59.093
And that's what he's talking about here.

00:20:01.085 --> 00:20:02.780
DGRA BYAS PA NA'ANG

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.640
Even if somebody comes
up to threaten the bird

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:06.699
with a DBYUG PA

00:20:06.699 --> 00:20:08.570
DDBYUG PA here means Danda.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:11.038
This is the Tibetan for Danda,

00:20:11.038 --> 00:20:14.700
DBYUG PA

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.110
If someone comes up with a
club and threatens to beat,

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.044
to kill the bird,

00:20:20.044 --> 00:20:22.890
DGRA BYAS PA, RANG NYID
GSHOG PA YOD PAS 'PHUR NUS

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:25.540
the mother herself RANG NYID

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:28.150
has a GSHOG PA, she has wings.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:30.600
She can 'PHUR, she can fly away.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:34.290
MOD, but she stays and she covers the baby

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:36.995
with her body.

00:20:36.995 --> 00:20:38.868
BU LA GCES NAS

00:20:38.868 --> 00:20:42.587
Here's the GCES in RANG GCES 'DZIN, Tim,

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.303
in Seiji's text, which is?

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.344
Self-cherishing. BU LA GCES

00:20:51.344 --> 00:20:52.770
to the child.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.229
She has self-cherishing for the child.

00:20:55.229 --> 00:20:56.062
SROG KYANG YAL BAR DOR

00:20:56.062 --> 00:20:56.895
YAL BAR DOR

00:20:56.895 --> 00:21:00.273
means she will abandon her own life,

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:06.993
if it means protecting her
child, even a bird will do that.

00:21:06.993 --> 00:21:07.826
SRUNG

00:21:07.826 --> 00:21:10.800
PHYIS NAS 'BU GCIG LAS MA RNYED

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:14.030
If you both survive long enough

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:18.482
and she can't find food
and she finds one worm.

00:21:18.482 --> 00:21:20.832
And here's our picture.

00:21:25.282 --> 00:21:27.414
'BU GCIG LAS MA RNYED means,

00:21:27.414 --> 00:21:30.088
even if she finds a single worm,

00:21:30.088 --> 00:21:33.419
if she can't find any
more than a single worm,

00:21:33.419 --> 00:21:36.496
BYIN NAS GSO BAR BYED

00:21:36.496 --> 00:21:37.990
she will give it to her child.

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:41.190
She will give it to the
baby and she will go hungry.

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:42.450
A mother bird will go hungry

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:46.903
and she will give the
single bug to her kid.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.620
What's this GSO mean, you guys, you nuns,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.896
she will GSO the child with the bug.

00:21:56.631 --> 00:22:00.050
Rakshita

00:22:00.050 --> 00:22:03.600
Sustain, and this is the GSO in GSO SBYONG

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.890
which means to fix your
vows or sustain your vows.

00:22:08.723 --> 00:22:13.080
DE LTAR YANG THANG RE
THANG GNYIS TZAM MIN PAR

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:16.120
It's not that your mom
did that for you once.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:19.502
It's not that your mom
protected you once or twice

00:22:19.502 --> 00:22:21.841
at the cost of her life.

00:22:21.841 --> 00:22:25.157
GNAS SKABS THAMS CAD DU
DRIN GYIS BSKYANGS PA LA

00:22:25.157 --> 00:22:26.615
GRANGS KYIS MI CHOD

00:22:26.615 --> 00:22:29.740
Every single lifetime
that she was your mom,

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:31.530
she protected you,

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:35.433
and you cannot count the
times that she died for you.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:40.045
SNGAR MGO LOG GI JAG PA ZHIG

00:22:40.045 --> 00:22:41.420
MGO LOG is a place

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.143
in East Tibet, famous for bandits.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:47.600
And MGO LOG

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:50.743
which is a tribal name
almost means bandit.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:56.120
JAG PA means a bandit,

00:22:56.120 --> 00:23:01.120
someone who robs travelers.
That's their specialty.

00:23:03.398 --> 00:23:04.231
JAG PA

00:23:04.231 --> 00:23:05.872
RTA ZHIG LA GRI BTAB PA

00:23:05.872 --> 00:23:07.120
They were having a fight,

00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:11.810
he stabbed somebody's
horse with a big knife.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:14.519
LTO BA LA THEBS TE

00:23:14.519 --> 00:23:16.953
RTE 'U means a baby horse.

00:23:16.953 --> 00:23:19.283
What do you call?

00:23:19.283 --> 00:23:21.322
A foal.

00:23:21.322 --> 00:23:22.530
LHUNG BA

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:23.690
This is the LHUNG BA

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:26.145
that you see in the monks and nuns vows,

00:23:26.145 --> 00:23:27.875
which means downfall.

00:23:27.875 --> 00:23:30.030
@THUNG BA or LHUNG BA

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:34.690
So what happened to the
colt or foal? LHUNG BA

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:37.480
It fell out of the womb.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:41.680
The bandit cut open the
stomach of another guy's horse,

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.430
And then a baby, foal came out, called,

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:47.709
SHI KHA MA

00:23:47.709 --> 00:23:50.273
means in the throes of death.

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:53.760
While she was dying,

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:57.060
the mother horse was licking the baby

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:59.120
as she was dying. She was worried

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:02.040
that the baby wouldn't
be clean or something.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.943
So she's dying and she's licking the baby.

00:24:04.943 --> 00:24:06.210
RTE 'U LA LCES BLDAG

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:08.630
There's the same LCES BLDAG we had before.

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:11.206
Lick with her tongue.

00:24:11.206 --> 00:24:12.410
BYAMS PA

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:14.960
she had BYAMS PA for the foal.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:16.480
She showed her love.

00:24:18.356 --> 00:24:22.398
MTHONG NAS, when the JAG PA MTHONGed that,

00:24:22.398 --> 00:24:27.340
he GCOD PAed, RDO RJE
GCOD PA his JAG PA LAS,

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:32.106
his career as a bandit.

00:24:32.106 --> 00:24:34.542
He stopped his career as a bandit.

00:24:34.542 --> 00:24:38.320
And he said, "God, this is me,
now I can't do this anymore."

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:40.710
So I guess in Tibet, it's a big deal.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.430
I guess it's like a car salesman

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:45.670
starting to tell the truth or something.

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:48.400
But he's like, "I can't do this anymore."

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:50.090
He gave up his JAG LAS

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:52.443
his bandit career.

00:24:54.369 --> 00:24:55.290
GCOD

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:57.860
He gave it up BYUNG BA RED

00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:02.740
Pabongka Rinpoche loves
to tell little stories

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:04.570
that he's heard around Tibet.

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:07.660
And I think if you're teaching Lam Rim,

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:09.882
you should also tell your own stories.

00:25:09.882 --> 00:25:12.540
That's part of the tradition.

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:15.320
Whatever stories happen in your life

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:17.440
that you can use to
illustrate the Lam Rim,

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:21.180
like the Mongolian wild kids story.

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.423
Then use it, it's okay, it's good.

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.630
It makes it more real for your audience.

00:25:26.630 --> 00:25:31.343
They don't have robbers on
horseback to talk about, I hope.

00:25:33.763 --> 00:25:36.262
DE'I DUS KYI RTE 'U DE RANG YIN LA

00:25:36.262 --> 00:25:38.929
What if that baby horse was you?

00:25:40.425 --> 00:25:43.090
What if the baby horse
that the MGO LOG bandit

00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:47.413
chieftain saw getting linked was you?

00:25:48.905 --> 00:25:52.655
RTA DE GZHAN 'DI MIN PA'I NGES PA CI YOD

00:25:53.845 --> 00:25:55.530
MIN NGES PA CI YOD

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.100
means there's no certainty at all,

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:01.590
that you were not that baby horse.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:03.610
You yourself was not the baby horse

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:05.270
that Pabongka Rinpoche is talking about.

00:26:05.270 --> 00:26:09.850
There's no certainty that you
and I were not the baby horse.

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:14.030
And there's no certainty
that someone around you now

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:18.060
was not the mother dying
horse who licked you.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.720
There's no certainty that
we are not the horses

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:24.990
that Pabongka Rinpoche is
describing in his story.

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:28.198
CI YOD, DE LTAR TSE 'DI'I
MAS RANG LA DRIN GYIS

00:26:28.198 --> 00:26:30.156
JI LTAR BSKYANGS PA LTAR

00:26:30.156 --> 00:26:34.410
So, we've established
that your mom struggled

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:36.920
to take care of you
financially, emotionally,

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:41.756
educationally, logistically in this life.

00:26:41.756 --> 00:26:43.680
SEMS CAN THAMS CAD KYIS KYANG DRIN GYIS

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.872
DE BZHIN DU BSKYANGS PAR BSAM

00:26:45.872 --> 00:26:50.410
You have to contemplate how
every other living being

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.150
has also done the same for you,

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:56.732
in many lifetimes, in countless lifetimes.

00:26:56.732 --> 00:26:59.740
It's a sweet meditation to do.

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:02.260
It's a sweet practice to do.

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.740
You know we, sometimes we feel depressed.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.670
I was depressed yesterday, I was sick.

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:10.677
Stuff happens in our life

00:27:10.677 --> 00:27:14.790
and we feel kind of sad about life

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:16.910
or things aren't going so well.

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:20.710
And it's always a nice,
you have the option

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:23.840
if you're a Lam Rim participant,

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:27.230
if you've studied Lam Rim,
or if you've heard Lam Rim,

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:28.880
you always have the option to just sit

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:32.870
and think about the
horse and the baby horse,

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:36.870
and to think that everybody
around you has licked you

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:38.820
as their baby horse.

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:42.530
And I think you'll find that
this Lam Rim meditations

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:44.900
will make you very cheerful

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:46.760
and grateful to other people.

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:49.870
So then the Lam Rim
has an immediate effect

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.760
when you're feeling depressed,

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.950
or you feel low self-esteem,

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:58.930
just imagine getting licked
by everybody in the room,

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:00.870
when you were a baby horse.

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:04.440
And it's true, you were, and
try to cheer up a little bit.

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:07.580
I mean, we have all
taken care of each other.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:09.230
We have all given up our lives

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:10.900
for each other countless times.

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:14.307
So, life is not so serious
and life is not so bad.

00:28:15.341 --> 00:28:17.591
See you in the next Lam Rim.

